    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d476">

                <pb facs="0506" n="406" xml:id="pg406"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760c.61/362: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Poland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gibson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Warsaw</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-09-29T20:00:00+02:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 29,
                                1920—8 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received October 3—9:52
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>534. My telegram number 533, September 29, 4 p.m.<anchor n="35" xml:id="d476-f1"
                    /> Farman<anchor n="36" xml:id="d476-f2"/> returned this evening from Slonim
                    front reports present operations now practically concluded have deprived
                    Bolsheviki of offensive possibilities for some time to come. States that both
                    officers and men [frankly] longing for immediate peace. This confirms
                    information that war weary public will support Sapieha’s efforts to conclude
                    peace without delay. Nothing but clever maneuvering by small war party [is now
                    visible] step as against peace.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gibson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
